// oracle.mn — the oracle dissolved: ranking projection over one search
//
// The oracle is NOT a subsystem. It is the kernel's two operations
// (draw-edge / project) run in three modes — the IC cursor (cached
// read), the e-graph (search), multi-shot (fork) — FUSED. PLAN §2:
// "incrementality (the oracle's IC) = cached cursor; multi-shot
// exploration = forked cursor; the oracle FUSES all three." So there
// is no separate cache, no separate search, no separate exploration
// substrate here. This file is the THIN RANKING LAYER that reads the
// queue live off the graph and orders it.
//
// Three things the graph/repo already proves, carried (not re-derived):
//   - the SEARCH is mentl.mn's gradient_next / try_each_annotation —
//     checkpoint -> apply -> verify -> rollback, already working.
//   - the SATURATION is egraph.mn's saturate / egraph_extract — the live
//     graph's union-find as a fabric EDGE (the canon weave, graph_canon_set/
//     graph_canon_at), pointed at the cheaper node, effect-aware (a dropping
//     rewrite fires only when the dropped operand's row subsumes the pure row).
//   - the CACHE is graph_handler's epoch (graph.mn) + the egraph
//     union-find — not a third sorted-list. The queue is a DERIVED
//     PROJECTION (every QueueItem is a proven-and-rolled-back
//     Annotation), memoized BY EPOCH, never a stored side-ledger.
//
// The build IS a `<~` feedback loop: source change -> graph_mutated
// fires at the outermost commit -> the IC fixpoint re-projects -> the
// project queue refreshes -> the cursor surfaces the highest-priority
// next-step -> user acts -> loop. Silence ONLY when the queue is empty
// — the gradient's top, the proven-complete state.
//
// Authority: docs/SYNTAX.md (surface) · PLAN.md §2 (the kernel) ·
// src/mentl.mn (the proposer — gradient_next, try_each_annotation,
// apply_annotation_tentatively, verify_after_apply, why_from_handle) ·
// src/egraph.mn (saturate / egraph_extract) · src/graph.mn (graph_handler:
// epoch, span_index, graph_push_checkpoint/rollback,
// graph_mutated at the outermost commit boundary).
import types

import effects

import mentl

import infer

import graph

import egraph

import combinators

import verify

import query

// ─── IC — the iterative context the `<~` fixpoint reads ──────────────
//
// IC is the Iterate-class effect (SYNTAX.md §`<~`: `Iterate`-class is an
// open class, not a name-allowlist) the build's `<~ accumulate(...)`
// fixpoint runs under. It is a row MARKER: its presence in the enclosing
// `~>` stack IS the iterative context the `<~` verb requires (the same
// way `Sample`/`Tick`/`Clock` mark theirs). `accumulate(init)` is the
// state-element; the IC handler re-projects on each graph delta
// (graph_mutated bumps the epoch) and converges when the projection stops
// changing — silence at the gradient's top. One effect, project-wide:
// cursor.mn / format.mn / mentl.mn / pipeline.mn run their fixpoints
// under it (the row carries the context; no op is performed by hand —
// the iteration IS the `<~` loop closing).
effect IC {
  
}

// ─── QueueItem — one proven candidate in the oracle's queue ──────────
//
// QueueItem — one proven candidate Mentl could surface. Each carries
// the position the candidate applies to, the candidate itself (an
// Annotation per src/mentl.mn), the capability it would unlock, an
// OraclePriority for ranking, the proof-chain Reason that justified
// inclusion, and the module that owns it. Surfaces query the queue
// and render the highest-priority item visible at the cursor or
// project-wide.
//
// Primitives: #2 (Propose — the candidate); #6 (Verify — the proof);
// #7 (Teach — the gradient direction); #8 (Reason — the chain).
type QueueItem
  = QueueItem({candidate: Annotation, capability: Capability, impact: OraclePriority, position: Int, reason: Reason})

// graph Handle where item applies
//
// what to add (per src/mentl.mn Annotation)
//
// what it unlocks (per src/mentl.mn Capability)
//
// OraclePriority — ranking dimension, every input READ LIVE from the
// graph (Carried-Truth: no stored counts). The priority function
// composes tier (compile-blocking first) + intent_weight (read live
// off the position's authored intent row) +
// surface_area (count of dependent positions, walked over graph edges)
// + locality_bias (byte distance from cursor, read from span_index).
// Total order via priority_lt: "compile-blocking always wins; among
// same-tier, intent-weight wins; among same-intent, surface-area wins;
// among same-surface, locality wins."
type OraclePriority = OraclePriority({intent_weight: Int, locality_bias: Int, surface_area: Int, tier: PriorityTier})

// 0-100; higher = more weighted
//
// count of dependent positions (live edge walk)
//
// byte distance from cursor (span_index); 0 = exact
//
// PriorityTier — five tiers in compile-impact order. TBlocking is the
// fix-this-or-nothing-compiles tier (always wins). TViolation is
// declared-row contradicted (E_PurityViolated, E_OwnershipViolation,
// E_EffectMismatch). TPending is V_Pending refinement obligation not
// yet discharged. TUnlock is gradient capability available (`with
// !Alloc`, `with Pure`, refinement narrowing). TStyle is topology nudge
// (use `|>` instead of nested calls — read from the e-graph's recorded
// cheaper-form equivalences). H6 exhaustive.
//
// This is NOT a duplicate of a DiagKind severity band — types.mn
// carries no severity/tier on its diagnostics, so PriorityTier is the
// one home of the oracle's compile-impact order (the audit per the
// adversarial verdict: no parallel enumeration exists to dissolve into).
type PriorityTier = TBlocking | TViolation | TPending | TUnlock | TStyle

// ─── OracleQuery effect — surfaces read the queue (read-only) ────────
//
// OracleQuery — read-only queries from surfaces. The queue is a live
// projection of the graph, re-derived (memoized by epoch) on read;
// surfaces never mutate it. Five surfaces subscribe:
// - voice (cursor-time MV) — query_project_queue + cursor_relevant
// - doc batch surface — query_module_queue per decl
// - LSP hover — query_at_position(cursor_handle)
// - teach CLI surface — query_at_position OR query_project_queue
// - Web playground — same as LSP, push on queue change
effect OracleQuery {
  // at this Handle
  query_at_position(Int) -> [QueueItem]
  // merged from all positions
  query_project_queue() -> [QueueItem]
  // for one module
  query_module_queue(String) -> [QueueItem]
  // (cursor_handle, max_distance_bytes); locality-filtered
  cursor_relevant(Int, Int) -> [QueueItem]
}

// ─── project_queue_merger — the queue projected live, memoized by epoch ─
//
// project_queue_merger handles every OracleQuery op the surfaces
// perform (voice.mn performs query_project_queue / cursor_relevant).
// The project-wide queue is a DERIVED PROJECTION of the graph — it is
// not stored; it is re-computed by running the proposer over the
// positions and ranking the survivors. The one cache is the graph
// EPOCH (graph.mn): if the epoch is unchanged since the last
// projection, the graph has not changed, so the queue is unchanged and
// the memoized projection is returned. This is the IC semantics — "read
// live is the meaning, cached-by-epoch is the mechanism" (PLAN §2) —
// with the graph's own epoch as the single truth, never a third cache.
handler project_queue_merger with last_epoch = -1, projected = [] {
  query_project_queue() => // query_project_queue — the live project queue, memoized by epoch.
  // Epoch unchanged -> the graph is unchanged -> return the projection.
  // Epoch changed -> re-project (run the proposer, rank survivors).
  {
    // drift-audit: ignore — effectful read into control-flow compare
    let epoch = graph_epoch()
    if epoch == last_epoch {
      resume(projected)
    } else {
      // drift-audit: ignore — effectful read used in both resume and with-state
      let queue = project_queue()
      resume(queue) with last_epoch = epoch, projected = queue
    }
  },
  query_at_position(handle) => // query_at_position — items in the queue that apply at this Handle.
  // Filters the project queue; cheap because items carry their position.
  resume(filter_at_position(query_project_queue(), handle)),
  query_module_queue(module_path) => // query_module_queue — items whose owning module matches.
  resume(filter_by_module(query_project_queue(), module_path)),
  cursor_relevant(cursor_handle, max_distance_bytes) => // cursor_relevant — locality-filtered items. Surfaces bias toward the
  // cursor's region (LSP hover, inline hints) and fall back to project-
  // wide priority when the cursor's region is silent. The fallback
  // decision lives in the surface (voice), not here.
  resume(filter_by_locality(query_project_queue(), cursor_handle, max_distance_bytes)),
}

// ─── project_queue — the proposer's survivors, ranked ────────────────
//
// project_queue — re-derive the project-wide queue from the live graph.
// Walk every position with dependents, run mentl.mn's existing proposer
// (try_each_annotation, already checkpoint->apply->verify->rollback) at
// each, wrap each survivor as a QueueItem, sort by descending priority.
// No separate search, no separate cache: the proposer IS the search,
// the graph epoch IS the cache. The e-graph supplies the TStyle-tier
// equivalences (topology nudges) effect-awarely via egraph.mn.
fn project_queue() with Verify + GraphRead + WASI + GraphWrite + Intern + Memory + Alloc + EnvRead = {
  // The performed-name union is a LINK fact — read once here, threaded
  // to every position (the same vocabulary the Teach install memoizes).
  let performed = performed_names_of_link()
  decl_handles() |> map(candidates_at(performed, ??)) |> flatten |> sort_by_priority
}

// candidates_at — every proven Annotation at one position becomes a
// QueueItem. Reuses mentl.mn's try_each_annotation (the working
// checkpoint/verify/rollback proposer) — Carried-Truth: one search
// substrate. The candidate set is mentl.mn's gradient_candidates (ONE
// enumeration, two readers — the cursor's single-step pick and this
// full ranked queue); each survivor carries the e-graph-aware
// priority and the Why-walkable Reason mentl.mn already minted.
// Stage-shaped (the Stage Law): the link vocabulary is CONFIG, the
// position is the flowing DATUM — project_queue's map completes the
// hole per position.
fn candidates_at(performed, pos) with Verify + GraphRead + GraphWrite + Alloc + Memory + Intern + EnvRead = {
  let node = graph_chase(pos)
  match node {
    GNode(kind, anchor) => match kind {
      NBound(ty) => try_each_annotation(pos, gradient_candidates(ty, performed), ty) |> map((pair) => proven_to_item(pos, pair, anchor)),
      _ => [],
    },
  }
}

// proven_to_item — wrap one (Annotation, Capability) survivor as a
// ranked QueueItem. The Reason is the position's anchor wrapped in the
// gradient suggestion's Located edge (the Why engine walks it via
// why_from_handle, mentl.mn) — no fabricated OracleVerified constructor;
// the proof chain mentl.mn already minted is carried.
fn proven_to_item(pos, pair, anchor) with GraphRead + Memory + Alloc = match pair {
  (ann, cap) => QueueItem({candidate: ann, capability: cap, impact: compute_priority(pos, ann), position: pos, reason: suggestion_reason(ann, anchor)}),
}

// ─── compute_priority — every input read LIVE from the graph ─────────
//
// compute_priority — rank a candidate by reading its inputs LIVE
// (Carried-Truth: no stored counts, no snapshot). tier from the
// annotation's gradient axis (the cursor's classification of what the
// candidate fixes); intent_weight from the position's authored intent
// row read LIVE off the FnStmt node (graph_node_body — never a parallel
// intent ledger); surface_area from a live walk of the positions
// that depend on this one (graph edges, not a count field); locality_bias
// from the span_index byte position. Reads `graph_snapshot` once for the
// span_index; the intent row is read per-handle from the node itself.
fn compute_priority(pos, ann) with GraphRead + Memory + Alloc = match graph_snapshot() {
  Graph(_next, span_index) => OraclePriority({intent_weight: intent_weight_at(pos), locality_bias: locality_at(span_index, pos), surface_area: surface_area_at(pos), tier: tier_of_annotation(ann)}),
}

// tier_of_annotation — the candidate's compile-impact tier, read from
// its gradient axis (suggestion_kind_of, mentl.mn). A row tightening
// that removes a declared-row contradiction is TUnlock; a refinement
// obligation is TPending; an ownership marker is TUnlock. The gradient
// kind IS the tier source — no parallel classification.
fn tier_of_annotation(ann) with Pure = match suggestion_kind_of(ann) {
  SuggestEffectRow => TUnlock,
  SuggestRefinement => TPending,
  SuggestOwnership => TUnlock,
  SuggestReturnType => TStyle,
}

// intent_weight_at — the position's authored intent row drives the
// weight: a position carrying an authored intent (e.g. a `!Alloc`
// real-time row) weights heavier than an un-annotated one. Read LIVE
// off the FnStmt node the handle names (graph_node_body) — the effs
// field IS the authored row; no parallel intent ledger to walk. A node
// that is not a fn declaration carries no authored intent -> baseline 0.
fn intent_weight_at(pos) with GraphRead + Memory = match graph_node_body(pos) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, effs, _)) => 50 + len(effs) * 10,
  _ => // drift-audit: ignore — the fn's own comment names it: a non-fn-decl node carries no authored intent, baseline 0
  0,
}

// surface_area_at — count of positions that depend on this one. For a
// named decl the dependents ARE the name's references, read from the
// graph's refs column (refs_of_name — the live projection whose
// zero-span skip and re-mint dedup make len the honest count). The
// former child-handle walk counted structural PARENTS — a FnStmt
// handle is no expression's child, so it answered a constant 0 and
// the rank input was dead.
fn surface_area_at(pos) with GraphRead + Memory + Alloc = match graph_node_body(pos) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(name, _, _, _, _)) => len(refs_of_name(name)),
  _ => // drift-audit: ignore — a position declaring no name has no name-keyed dependents, baseline 0
  0,
}

// node_contains_handle — TREE containment: is `target` the node at
// `h` or a descendant of it? The span form cannot answer this (decl
// and body spans are head-anchored — the measured 2026-07-28 hole);
// the tree IS the truth. Bounded by the subtree.
fn node_contains_handle(h, target) with GraphRead + Alloc + Memory + !Mutate = if h == target {
  true
} else {
  any((c) => node_contains_handle(c, target), body_child_handles(graph_node_body(h)))
}

// ── the verb-shape read — the topology the bindings already draw ─────
//
// SYNTAX ⌖: `|>` is NEVER optional on a chain — `y |> f |> g` over
// `let x = f(y); g(x)`. The verb is a PROJECTION of use-count (the
// same edge own/ref grades from): a let-bound name consumed EXACTLY
// ONCE, by exactly the FOLLOWING statement, is a `|>` stage written as
// a binding; a name used twice is `<|` territory and never fires; a
// single step is the law's own exception (the pipe wins on CHAINS).
// Counting walks the one total child projection — never a text scan.
//
// body_is_var — is this body exactly a VarRef of `name`? The counting
// walk's leaf test as its own projection (a non-VarRef body is simply
// not the name — the false arm is the predicate's definition).
fn body_is_var(body, name: String) with Pure = match body {
  NExpr(VarRef(n)) => n == name,
  _ => false,
}

// count_var_uses — VarRef occurrences of `name` in the subtree at h.
// An inner rebinding shadows and over-counts toward "more than one
// use", which only SUPPRESSES an invite — conservative by construction.
fn count_var_uses(h, name: String) with GraphRead + Alloc + Memory = {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  let here = if body_is_var(body, name) { 1 } else { 0 }
  here + (body_child_handles(body) |> map((c) => count_var_uses(c, name)) |> fold(0, (a, x) => a + x))
}

// chain_scan — walk a block's statement spine (stmts ++ final): a
// LetStmt(PVar) whose name has exactly one use, inside exactly the
// next node, extends the current run; anything else flushes it. Runs
// of length >= 2 are `|>` invites, reported as step counts.
// The spine's links, then the runs — two stages, neither threading an
// index through a self-call. A LINK is per-position (does stmt i feed
// stmt i+1 exactly once?); the runs are a fold over those links
// carrying (run, acc), flushing a run of >= 2 as one `|>` invite.
fn chain_scan(nodes) with GraphRead + Alloc + Memory = match chain_links(nodes) |> fold(
  (0, []),
  (state, link) => match state {
    (run, acc) => if link { (run + 1, acc) } else { (0, keep_run(run, acc)) },
  }
) {
  (run, acc) => keep_run(run, acc),
}

// A run of two or more bindings IS the chain the pipe draws; one step is
// the law's own exception.
fn keep_run(run, acc) with Alloc + Memory = if run >= 2 { acc ++ [run] } else { acc }

fn chain_links(nodes) with GraphRead + Alloc + Memory = if len(nodes) < 2 {
  []
} else {
  range(0, len(nodes) - 1) |> map((i) => match graph_node_body(node_handle(nodes[i])) {
    NStmt(LetStmt(PVar(name), _, _)) => let_feeds_next(name, nodes, i),
    _ => false,
  })
}

fn let_feeds_next(name: String, nodes, i) with GraphRead + Alloc + Memory =
  count_var_uses(node_handle(nodes[i + 1]), name) == 1 && rest_uses(name, nodes, i + 2) == 0

// The suffix's total use-count — a `|>` chain over the tail, not an
// index threaded through a self-call (iteration is topology; this is
// the tier's own neighbour, convicted by the tier it sits four lines
// above).
fn rest_uses(name: String, nodes, j) with GraphRead + Alloc + Memory =
  nodes |> drop(j) |> map((n) => count_var_uses(node_handle(n), name)) |> sum

// pipe_shape_of — the verb-shape read over a fn's body spine, the
// audit tier's entry (build_audit_report reads it per fn).
fn pipe_shape_of(fn_handle) with GraphRead + Alloc + Memory = match graph_node_body(fn_handle) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, _, body)) => match graph_node_body(node_handle(body)) {
    NExpr(BlockExpr(stmts, fin)) => chain_scan(stmts ++ [fin]),
    _ => [],
  },
  _ => [],
}

// ── the anonymity read — the named stage in hiding ───────────────────
//
// INTENT IS LOSSLESS (PLAN §0; the stamp Hβ.audit.anonymity-tier): an
// anonymous fn that already HAS a name (the eta-wrapper — its body is
// exactly a named call over its own params, so the name exists and the
// lambda hides it) or that CARRIES a row (a non-Pure lambda — the
// effects deserve a decl home with a `with` clause) is intent
// discarded at the site that knew it. The pure-local
// immediately-consumed lambda is the vocabulary and stays silent —
// `map((x) => x + 1, xs)` never narrates, whatever param it lands on.
// The escape class is DEP `Hβ.infer.use-profile` (band N): until the
// escape-bit lands, the read speaks the two classes it can prove.
fn anonymity_shape_of(fn_handle) with GraphRead + !Mutate + Alloc + Memory = match graph_node_body(fn_handle) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, _, body)) => lambda_shapes(node_handle(body), (0, 0)),
  _ => (0, 0),
}

fn lambda_shapes(h, acc) with GraphRead + !Mutate + Alloc + Memory = {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  let here = match body {
    NExpr(LambdaExpr(params, lbody)) => classify_lambda(h, params, lbody, acc),
    _ => acc,
  }
  body_child_handles(body) |> fold(here, (a, c) => lambda_shapes(c, a))
}

fn classify_lambda(h, params, lbody, acc) with GraphRead + !Mutate + Alloc + Memory = match acc {
  (eta, rowed) => if lambda_is_eta(params, lbody) {
    (eta + 1, rowed)
  } else if lambda_carries_row(h) {
    (eta, rowed + 1)
  } else {
    acc
  },
}

// locality_at — byte distance from the cursor, read from the graph's
// span_index ([(ValidSpan, Int)]). The position's span start IS the
// locality coordinate; 0 = at the cursor. Read live; absent -> far.
fn locality_at(span_index, pos) with Pure = match span_index {
  [] => 1000000,
  [(span, h), ...t] => if h == pos { span_start(span) } else { locality_at(t, pos) },
}

// The proposer's scope is the decls column's projection (decl_handles,
// query.mn — one home, two readers: this seed and the "decls" facet).
// The candidate enumeration is decl-shaped by construction
// (gradient_candidates_at's TFun arm over the decl's
// with-clause/ownership surface); the former whole-handle NBound walk
// also seeded every fn-typed MENTION — each re-enumerating its decl's
// own candidate set, N copies interleaved in the queue — and lambdas,
// which have no annotation surface.
//
// ─── oracle_ic_fixpoint — the `<~` build loop (Iterate-class IC) ─────
//
// oracle_ic_fixpoint — the `<~` feedback loop closing on queue
// stability. PLAN §2: build IS the `<~` loop; the oracle re-projects
// when the graph mutates (graph_mutated fires at the outermost commit),
// and converges when no QueueItem changes between cycles (silence at the
// gradient's top, silence_predicate_oracle).
//
// The `<~` runs under the IC effect — the Iterate-class context
// SYNTAX.md §`<~` requires (an iteration-resume effect, NOT a
// Mutate-event subscription; graph_mutated TRIGGERS invalidation but is
// not itself the iterative context). `accumulate(empty_queue())` is the
// canonical state-element: the queue starts empty and IC-narrows toward
// the proven-complete projection. project_queue_merger reads the queue
// live, memoized by epoch — one re-projection per real graph change.
//
// `mentl edit` IS this loop running. There is no separate `compile`:
// the edit IS the projection IS the loop closing.
fn oracle_ic_fixpoint() with OracleQuery + IC + Memory + Alloc =
  query_project_queue() ~> project_queue_merger <~ accumulate(empty_queue())

fn empty_queue() with Memory + Alloc = []

// ─── silence_predicate — the strong gradient-top gate ────────────────
//
// silence_predicate_oracle — Mentl is silent ONLY when the project
// queue is empty: the gradient's top, everything proven, nothing more
// to add. Until then she surfaces the highest-priority next step. The
// per-tentacle render-or-not decision lives in the surface (voice);
// this predicate gates the STRONG silence — no proven next-steps
// anywhere in the project.
fn silence_predicate_oracle(situation) with OracleQuery = match query_project_queue() {
  [] => // gradient at top — full silence
  true,
  [_, ..._] => // queue has items — Mentl surfaces something
  false,
}

// ─── Priority — total order over OraclePriority ──────────────────────
//
// priority_lt — total order. Tier wins first; among same-tier,
// intent_weight wins; among same-intent, surface_area wins; among
// same-surface, locality wins (closer to cursor wins).
fn priority_lt(a, b) with Pure = match a {
  OraclePriority(pa) => match b {
    OraclePriority(pb) => if !tier_eq(pa.tier, pb.tier) {
      tier_lt(pa.tier, pb.tier)
    } else if pa.intent_weight != pb.intent_weight {
      pa.intent_weight > pb.intent_weight
    } else if pa.surface_area != pb.surface_area {
      pa.surface_area > pb.surface_area
    } else {
      pa.locality_bias < pb.locality_bias
    },
  },
}

// priority_gt — descending order; just !priority_lt for non-equal.
fn priority_gt(a, b) with Pure = !priority_lt(a, b) && !priority_eq(a, b)

fn priority_eq(a, b) with Pure = match a {
  OraclePriority(pa) => match b {
    OraclePriority(pb) => tier_eq(pa.tier, pb.tier) && pa.intent_weight == pb.intent_weight && pa.surface_area == pb.surface_area && pa.locality_bias == pb.locality_bias,
  },
}

// tier_lt — total order over PriorityTier (TBlocking < TViolation <
// TPending < TUnlock < TStyle). H6 exhaustive 5-arm match via tier_rank.
fn tier_lt(a, b) with Pure = tier_rank(a) < tier_rank(b)

fn tier_eq(a, b) with Pure = tier_rank(a) == tier_rank(b)

fn tier_rank(t) with Pure = match t {
  TBlocking => 0,
  TViolation => 1,
  TPending => 2,
  TUnlock => 3,
  TStyle => 4,
}

// ─── Pure list combinators over the queue ────────────────────────────
fn qitem_position(item) with Pure = match item {
  QueueItem(r) => r.position,
}

fn qitem_impact(item) with Pure = match item {
  QueueItem(r) => r.impact,
}

// sort_by_priority — order QueueItems by DESCENDING priority. Merge sort
// (stable, O(N log N)); the comparator is priority_gt over each item's
// impact. The flat survivor stream becomes the ranked queue.
fn sort_by_priority(items) with Memory + Alloc = {
  let n = len(items)
  if n <= 1 {
    items
  } else {
    let mid = n / 2
    merge_by_impact(sort_by_priority(slice(items, 0, mid)), sort_by_priority(slice(items, mid, n)))
  }
}

fn merge_by_impact(a, b) with Memory + Alloc = match (a, b) {
  ([], _) => b,
  (_, []) => a,
  ([ha, ...ta], [hb, ...tb]) => if priority_gt(qitem_impact(ha), qitem_impact(hb)) {
    [ha] ++ merge_by_impact(ta, b)
  } else {
    [hb] ++ merge_by_impact(a, tb)
  },
}

// filter_at_position — items whose candidate applies at this Handle.
fn filter_at_position(items, handle) with Memory + Alloc = match items {
  [] => [],
  [h, ...t] => if qitem_position(h) == handle {
    [h] ++ filter_at_position(t, handle)
  } else {
    filter_at_position(t, handle)
  },
}

// Keeps the queue items this module owns, reading each item's module LIVE
// from the node it already points at.
//
// Module-of-position is the module STAMPED ON THE ITEM'S OWN NODE at its mint
// (`graph_module_of`), never a field snapshotted at item construction (the
// Universal Audit — the stored module_path was a copy of a live fact).
//
// It read `module_path_of_span(parse_span_of(qitem_position(h)))`: a handle
// turned into a span so the span could be matched back to a module by
// CONTAINMENT over every NModule node. The item was holding the handle the
// whole time, and the containment answer stopped being reliable the moment
// each module's spans became its own 1-based coordinates — an NModule span
// covers lines 1..N of its file, so every module long enough to reach the
// line contains it and the first in mint order wins. `graph_module_of` is the
// column that ends that question, and its own comment calls this read its
// "O(1) destiny".
fn filter_by_module(items, module_path) with Memory + Alloc + GraphRead + !Mutate = match items {
  [] => [],
  [h, ...t] => if module_name_of_handle(graph_module_of(qitem_position(h))) == module_path {
    [h] ++ filter_by_module(t, module_path)
  } else {
    filter_by_module(t, module_path)
  },
}

// filter_by_locality — items within max_distance handles of the cursor.
// Handle distance is the locality proxy: handle order is parse order, so
// the delta tracks source proximity.
fn filter_by_locality(items, cursor_handle, max_distance) with Memory + Alloc = match items {
  [] => [],
  [h, ...t] => if handle_distance(qitem_position(h), cursor_handle) <= max_distance {
    [h] ++ filter_by_locality(t, cursor_handle, max_distance)
  } else {
    filter_by_locality(t, cursor_handle, max_distance)
  },
}

fn handle_distance(a, b) with Pure = if a >= b { a - b } else { b - a }

// ─── Connection to the kernel ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Primitive #1 (Graph + Env) — the oracle reads from the SAME graph
// inference writes; the queue is a live projection (positions from the
// program weave, priority inputs from intent_index/span_index/overlays),
// memoized by the graph's own epoch. No oracle-resident state but the
// epoch memo.
//
// Primitive #2 (Handlers + resume) — project_queue_merger is the one
// handler; the search is mentl.mn's proposer (try_each_annotation), the
// saturation egraph.mn's. No parallel search mechanism.
//
// Primitive #3 (Five verbs) — project_queue is `|> map |> flatten |>
// sort`; the build is `query_project_queue ~> project_queue_merger <~
// accumulate(...)` (the IC fixpoint). The cycle is visible on the page.
//
// Primitive #4 (Effect row) — project_queue performs `Verify +
// GraphRead + GraphWrite` (the proposer's checkpoint/verify/rollback;
// the e-graph's saturation is GraphRead/GraphWrite now — the canon weave
// IS the graph, no separate EGraph effect); OracleQuery is read-only.
//
// Primitive #5 (Ownership) — the queue `own`s its items; surfaces read
// via `ref` (OracleQuery returns lists they do not consume).
//
// Primitive #6 (Refinement) — every QueueItem survived
// verify_after_apply (mentl.mn) before entering the queue; unproven
// candidates never reach a surface.
//
// Primitive #7 (Gradient) — the oracle IS the gradient's runtime: each
// QueueItem.candidate is one gradient step; the cursor shows the
// highest-priority one; silence ONLY at gradient top.
//
// Primitive #8 (Reason) — each QueueItem carries the proof chain
// mentl.mn minted (suggestion_reason -> Located); the Why engine walks
// it via why_from_handle. No fabricated Reason constructor.
//
// Mentl tentacle mapping: Query reads state at the cursor; Propose
// surfaces QueueItem candidates; Topology proposes `|>` rewrites
// (TStyle, from the e-graph's recorded equivalences); Unlock surfaces
// TUnlock items; Trace surfaces TViolation items; Verify surfaces
// TPending items; Teach surfaces the highest-priority TUnlock at the
// cursor; Why walks the Reason chain. One queue, eight filtered views.
//
// ─── What this substrate refuses ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// - A separate cache. The queue is memoized by the graph EPOCH (one
//   truth) — never a sorted-list side-ledger alongside the epoch and the
//   e-graph union-find.
// - A separate search. The proposer is mentl.mn's try_each_annotation;
//   the saturation egraph.mn's. The oracle ranks; it does not re-derive
//   speculation.
// - A stored position/queue ledger. Positions and priority inputs are
//   read live from the program weave + intent_index/span_index. Every
//   QueueItem is a proven-and-rolled-back Annotation, derived on read.
// - Subscribing to Mentl's own speculative writes. graph_mutated fires
//   only at outermost commit boundaries (graph.mn); speculative writes
//   between checkpoint/rollback never reach the IC fixpoint.
// - Surfaces mutating oracle state. OracleQuery is read-only.
//
// Mentl solves Mentl. The oracle is the IC cursor + the e-graph +
// multi-shot — the kernel's two operations in three modes, ranked. No
// new substrate; one projection, memoized by epoch.

